  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 1 card dq reg: grade 7.8: "Forces act within and between particles. How do they explain melting point, conductivity and hardness?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 3 s03-dq: grade 7.8: "Forces act within and between particles. How do they explain melting point, conductivity and hardness?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 6 s06-rule: grade 7.8: "Bonds hold atoms or ions together in a substance. Much weaker attractions act between molecules."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 7 s07-rule: grade 7.4: "Dispersion forces act between all molecules. More electrons make stronger dispersion forces. Stronger forces mean a higher boiling point."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 8 s08-rule: grade 7.6: "Polar molecules pull each other. These pulls are dipole-dipole forces. They keep dispersion forces too."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 9 s09-rule: grade 4.3 (outside 5 to 8): "Hydrogen bonding needs an H atom bonded to N, O or F. The next molecule needs a lone pair on N, O or F."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 11 s11-rule: grade 7.4: "Melting and boiling separate molecules. They do not break the covalent bonds inside them."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 12 s12-rule: grade 3 (outside 5 to 8): "Hydrogen bonds hold ice in open rings. So ice is less dense than liquid water, and it floats."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 15 s15-rule: grade 5.9: "A substance conducts when it has charged particles that can move. Ions in a liquid or a solution. Delocalized electrons in a metal."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 17 s17-rule: grade 4 (outside 5 to 8): "Some ionic and some molecular substances dissolve in water. Some do not. The type of bond alone does not predict it."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 18 s18-rule: grade 5.8: "A metal bends because its electrons move with the shifting cations. An ionic crystal splits because shifted layers put like charges side by side."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 19 s19-rule: grade 5.8: "In a covalent network solid, covalent bonds join every atom to its neighbors. To melt it, covalent bonds must break. So it melts only when very hot."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 22 card prompt reg: grade 4.9 (outside 5 to 8): "Methane and water both have 10 electrons. Methane boils at −161.5 °C, water at 100.0 °C. Explain the difference."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 22 s22-sample: grade 4.9 (outside 5 to 8): "Water forms hydrogen bonds: H on O to lone pairs. Methane has dispersion forces only, so it boils lower."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 card prompt reg: grade 2.1 (outside 5 to 8): "Solid salt leaves the bulb dark. Salt water lights it. Explain both with the particles."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 s23-sample: grade 3.1 (outside 5 to 8): "Solid salt has Na + and Cl − ions, locked in the crystal. In salt water, ions move and carry the current."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 card prompt reg: grade 2.4 (outside 5 to 8): "A hammer bends a copper sheet but splits a salt crystal. Explain both."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 s24-sample: grade 4.3 (outside 5 to 8): "Copper's electrons move with the sliding cations, so it bends. In salt, shifted layers put like charges side by side; they repel."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 25 card prompt dq reg: grade 7.8: "Forces act within and between particles. How do they explain melting point, conductivity and hardness?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 25 s25-sample: grade 4.7 (outside 5 to 8): "The forces that come apart decide the melting point. Snow, 0 °C: only attractions between molecules let go. Salt, 801 °C: ionic bonds break. Sand, near 1700 °C: covalent bonds break. Attractions are weaker, so snow melts lowest."
